  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 950 PM MDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\nThe pattern remains essentially the same. High pressure to our\nsouth is maintaining a persistent flow of hot and dry air\nthrough the CWA. The center of the high is close enough overhead\nthat the strongest upper-level flow isn't making it to our\nregion, somewhat hampering winds and gusts. However, some mild\nenhancement in said upper-level flow dips down across the\nnorthern and central CWA Thursday, and with clear skies in the \nforecast supporting strong mixing, this should be enough to \nprompt some 25-30 mph gusts in some locations and yet another \nday of scattered critical fire weather concerns. On Thursday, \nthese concerns will generally be confined to the Gunnison River \nBasin and northern portions of the San Juans. As the high lifts \nsubtly northwards Friday and into the weekend, winds die off \nonce more, leading to a low probability in critical fire weather\nconditions aside from very localized areas. This does coincide \nwith another heat-up though, raising valley locations back to \n10 degrees above normal, which will once again threaten to break\nhigh temperature records. We might see some changes next week \nas models are hinting at some moisture sneaking up underneath \nthe high bringing more isolated to scattered showers and storms.",
